!RISC3D: RISC OS's 3D driver interface
======================================

RISC3D lets programs that draw with Mesa (OpenGL, OpenGL ES) use the
machine's GPU. Programs don't call it themselves: Mesa does. With no GPU
driver, Mesa draws in software as before.

What's in here
--------------
  RISC3D   the module: the interface between Mesa and the GPU driver
  GPU      the GPU drivers. Each starts only on the machine it is for,
           so the same !RISC3D serves every machine:
             Linux3D    RISC OS on Linux (Linux's graphics driver draws)
             V3DRender  Raspberry Pi 4 / 400 / CM4 (native RISC OS)
           with their licences (V3DRender is GPL-2.0-or-later: GPU.Licences)

!RISC3D belongs in !Boot.Resources, where it is seen at start-up.
Install and update it with PackMan (package RISC3D), on every system.

For programs
------------
Nothing: !RISC3D loads RISC3D at start-up, and RISC3D loads the right
driver from GPU itself (it tries each module there until one starts; a
driver refuses to start where its GPU isn't there). *RISC3D says which
driver is in use, and what was tried. A program that must have RISC3D
can check in its !Run:
  RMEnsure RISC3D 0.05 Error This program needs !RISC3D

A program whose contexts share objects (one context per thread, as
WebKit's Skia draws) needs RISC3D 0.06; on RISC OS on Linux, Linux3D 0.06
and Linux's riscos-risc3d 0.23-1 or later. *RISC3D says "contexts can
share objects" when they can.

Configure's RISC3D window shows the driver and what it offers, and can
turn RISC3D's GPU drawing off (RISC3D 0.08 loads no driver while
RISC3D$Off is set; the choice is kept in Choices:RISC3D.Settings).

For testing, *Set RISC3D$Drivers to another folder to load drivers from
there instead of GPU.

